KZ3 patch 2141 (RSX pass 134): the PSO preload's key file is tied to the shader translator that wrote it.
Design and code from the Killzone 2 port (Project Pyrrhus, its patch 6047); both projects are MIT and have the same maintainer.

The preload (patch 2031) records, per PSO key (guest microcode + render state), the hashes of the HLSL the translator
produced, rebuilds those pipelines from the compiled blobs at the next start, and the walker then uses a preloaded PSO by
key alone. The key file's header checked only its layout and SHADER_DISK_CACHE_VERSION, so a build updated over an older
install could run the previous build's translated shaders against its own root signature, constants and bindings. The
Killzone 2 port showed this under Proton: an exe whose fragment translator writes magenta filled a cache, and the
unchanged exe on that cache drew a 100 % magenta frame.

Fix: libs/video/rsx_pso_keys_hdr.h (pure C, host test tools/smoke/test_pso_keys_hdr.sh): header version 3 with the
translator id RSX_TRANSLATOR_ID (the first 16 hex digits of a SHA-256 over the names and hashes of libs/video/*.c and *.h,
defined for rsx_live_draw.c by port/kz3/CMakeLists.txt at configure time; 0 when a build does not define it). A key file
from another layout or another translator is deleted together with the pipeline library beside it, with one
[pso-preload] line; the compiled shader blobs are keyed by their HLSL text and stay. Header layout: 32 bytes,
id_lo at byte offset 24, id_hi at 28.

Rebased on ps3recomp v0.19.0 (SPU pass 154): three-way merge into the v0.19.0 chain, hunks kept as before, context follows v0.19 (chain glue, owner lane not re-reviewed).

